        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Acting theory is the systematic study of how performance is produced — how an actor creates a character, maintains consistency across performances, and produces an audience experience. Modern acting theory begins with Stanislavski's systematic approach (late 19th c.) and has branched into multiple traditions ranging from the strictly psychological (Method) to the rigorously physical (Lecoq, Suzuki) to the technique-focused (Meisner)."
    },
    {
      "id": "05-ACTING-THEORY.md#003",
      "path": [
        "05-ACTING-THEORY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:05-acting-theory, git-history:theater-performance:05-acting-theory]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/05-ACTING-THEORY.md",
        "concepts": "[acting, theory]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/05-ACTING-THEORY.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:acting-theory",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[acting, theory]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Acting Theory and Training",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|           ACTING THEORY — LANDSCAPE                              |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  STANISLAVSKI SYSTEM (1860-1938)                                 |\n|  The founding framework: given circumstances, magic \"if,\"       |\n|  units and objectives, through-line, emotional memory           |\n|                                                                  |\n|  AMERICAN DERIVATIVES                                            |\n|  Method Acting (Strasberg): emotional memory as primary tool    |\n|  Meisner Technique (Meisner): \"living truthfully in imaginary   |\n|  circumstances\"; listening and reacting                          |\n|  Stella Adler (imagination over memory)                         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  PHYSICAL/EXTERNAL APPROACHES                                    |\n|  Meyerhold's Biomechanics (1920s): physical score, reflex arc  |\n|  Lecoq (neutral mask, physical comedy, bouffon)                 |\n|  Suzuki Method: lower body strength, physical presence          |\n|  Michael Chekhov (psychological gesture, atmosphere)            |\n|                                                                  |\n|  BRECHTIAN/GESTURAL APPROACH                                     |\n|  Social gesture (Gestus): not inner emotion but class position  |\n|  embodied in physical behavior                                   |\n|  Anti-empathy, demonstrating rather than being                  |\n|                                                                  |\n|  VOICE AND SPEECH                                                |\n|  Linklater, Berry: freeing the natural voice                    |\n|  Classical text: scansion, verse speaking, rhetoric             |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
